## Betaflight

    Upstream    https://github.com/betaflight/betaflight
    Licence     GPLv3
    Vendored as a git submodule at vendor/betaflight, unmodified; every
    change is a patch file in patches/ applied at build time.

The Betaflight logo, assets/credits/betaflight.svg, and the one colour
assets/credits/betaflight-mono.svg that scripts/partners.js makes from it,
are the Betaflight project's mark and not under this project's GPLv3 grant.
Betaflight holds trade marks in the name and the logo in the major markets.

Betaflight gave the owner permission on 2026-09-27 to use the Betaflight
name and logo in the game, because WebFPV is free. They also said the
project has nothing to do with WebFPV and will not publicise it. So
Betaflight is never called a partner, a sponsor or a supporter, and nothing
says or implies they endorse WebFPV. The logo is used in the game only: in
the credits, and under "Powered by" at the foot of the loading screen,
which is a statement about the code, because the control loop is Betaflight
compiled to WebAssembly. It stood beside the partners on the front door and
the board for a day and the owner took it off both, because those are the
pages that publicise the project. The board's credits roll mirrors these
credits as their fallback, and on the owner's word it names and links
Betaflight without the logo. The name still appears
in words wherever a page says what the control loop is, because that is a
fact about the code and the attribution the licence asks for. The owner
keeps the message itself.

## Music, and an open question

    Files       assets/music/, fourteen titles as mp3 and webm
    Named in    src/render/tracks.js
    Licence     NOT RECORDED. This entry exists to say so.

The music is served with a year long immutable cache and played on the menus
and in flight, so it is redistributed with the repository like any other file
in it. The repository is GPLv3. That makes the terms these recordings arrived
under a thing this file has to state, and nobody wrote them down: tracks.js
records an id and a display name per title and nothing else, the PROGRESS.md
entry that introduced them ("recorded tracks replace the generated bed",
2026-08-19) names no composer, and there is no other record in the tree.

Only the owner can close this, because only the owner knows where they came
from. Whichever it is, the answer belongs here:

  - the owner's own recordings, in which case say so and they are covered by
    the project's own GPLv3 grant;
  - a library or a generator, in which case name it and quote the licence
    that permits redistribution;
  - anything that does NOT permit redistribution, in which case the files
    come out of the repository and the crate loads from somewhere else.

Recorded rather than quietly left, because a silent gap in a licence file is
the kind of thing that is discovered by somebody else.

